A lock washer turns a tightened nut into a secured one. An inner tab sits in the keyway of the shaft or adapter sleeve so the washer cannot rotate, and one of the outer tabs is bent up into a slot in the lock nut so the nut cannot rotate either.

It is a small, inexpensive component with an outsized consequence when it is wrong or left out. A nut that works loose lets the bearing move on its taper, clearance opens up, and the bearing fails early.

We supply lock washers matched to our lock nut and sleeve ranges, with non-standard sizes made to requirement.
